<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#arras-culture">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arras culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400 BCE – 1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> culture is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> culture of East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, from about the 4th century BCE to the Roman conquest, named after a cemetery at Arras near Market Weighton. It is marked by burials under small square barrows, some containing dismantled two-wheeled vehicles, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetwang">Wetwang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garton-slack">Garton Slack</a>. The likeness of these rites to those of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> has fuelled debate over migration, and the culture is linked to the Parisi of Roman sources.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> culture belongs to the Middle Iron Age and is defined by its burials. Most graves are single inhumations under square barrows, the body usually laid on a north-south axis with the head to the north, extended or with the legs bent. Grave goods include metalwork, ceramics and animal remains, often pig and horse bones. Unlike the spread-out cemeteries of the continent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> cemeteries were crowded.</p><p>A few graves hold two-wheeled carts or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, usually taken apart, at sites such as Cawthorne Camps, Pexton Moor, Danes Graves, Garton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetwang">Wetwang</a>, Burton Fleming and Hornsea. Their small number, with high-quality metalwork and imported coral in the graves, suggests that those buried with chariots were a local elite. Excavation at Thornton has shown iron smelting well established in the area, probably supplying the tools and funerary goods these communities needed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, among them William Watson, the Rev E. W. Stillingfleet and Barnard Clarkson, dug more than a hundred barrows at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> in 1815–1817, either side of the road from Market Weighton to Beverley, now the A1079. Many of their records are lost, but four rich graves were recorded in detail: the King's Barrow, the Queen's Barrow, the Lady's Barrow and the Charioteer's Barrow. John Thurnam of the Yorkshire Antiquarian Club re-examined them in 1850.</p><p>The Lady's Barrow held a woman with a mirror under her head beside a dismantled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>; the Queen's Barrow, the only one of the four without a vehicle, held a coral brooch, a gold ring and a necklace of glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>. In 2017 excavations at Pocklington uncovered a chariot buried with two horses, dated to about 320 to 174 BCE, the first such grave found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with its horses, together with a well-preserved bronze shield whose boss resembles that of the Wandsworth shield.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The grave goods and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> designs are mainly British in style, though the burial rites resemble those of the continent and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a>. A study of 2026 found some evidence of matriclans, matriliny and matrilocality among the people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> culture, and no significant genetic link with the Celtic peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, so that the likeness is more likely due to cultural diffusion than to mass migration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arras culture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Parisii are identified with the <b>Arras culture</b> of the Yorkshire Wolds, which from about the 4th century BCE buried its dead under small square barrows, sometimes with dismantled two-wheeled vehicles, as at Wetwang and Garton Slack.</blockquote><a href="/state/parisii-britain">Parisii (Britain)</a></li></ul></section>
